      "writtenOn": "2024-01-09T01:03:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Can you explain the locking scheme used here? It looks like you have eliminated the node lock that the C version used and instead use the inner lock of the process that `owner` points to.",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2119c221_4af3a5c0",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 54,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-09T09:45:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve debated with myself several times whether I should take the time to remove this discrepancy. Currently, Rust uses these locks:\n\n* A mutex on process. Used only to protect the collection of nodes.\n* A spinlock on process. This also protects nodes owned by the process.\n* A spinlock on thread. This is separate from the process.\n\nI believe this exists due to a historical accident: the `LockedBy` utility needed for having a struct be protected by a lock in a different struct was probably not implemented yet when locks were added to threads.",
      "parentUuid": "b10b8add_0b99ddfc",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2721210f_24b3156f",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 74,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-03T00:59:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If this is for the debug_id in the C version of the code, then I think it should not be here. The debug_id is shared between object types to make it easier to read the debug information. Transactions, nodes and refs share the same debug id space so nodes and refs will have ids near the transaction that created them.",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a433847a_599c688d",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 74,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-04T11:11:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah, that makes sense. I\u0027ll rename it to `debug_id` and make it share the id space with other objects. (Or I might remove it, and add it back when I finish the binderfs debugging files.)",
      "parentUuid": "2721210f_24b3156f",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "de5fc0f5_3858a3b7",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 74,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-04T21:17:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The debug_id is used in debug printfs as well as the debug files. If you don\u0027t have either of those, you can leave it out until you need it.",
      "parentUuid": "a433847a_599c688d",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7d0bd4ee_54a32b11",
        "filename": "drivers/android/process.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 242,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-09T00:52:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This seems to replace the `outer_lock` in `struct binder_proc` from the C version. That lock is a spinlock though. The pre-fine-grained locking version of the driver used a mutex for it\u0027s global lock, but this was abandoned since a low priority thread holding that lock while it got preempted would cause glitches in high priority threads trying to get the same lock. Unless the linux mutex/rt_mutex implementation has improved since then, using a mutex here could cause a performance regression.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 242,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 242,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a9ba62c6_830e23bc",
        "filename": "drivers/android/process.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 242,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-09T09:45:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The driver used to allocate memory while holding this mutex. Not sure if that\u0027s still the case.",
      "parentUuid": "7d0bd4ee_54a32b11",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 242,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 242,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c0abb748_f6f096da",
        "filename": "drivers/android/process.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 421,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-03T02:16:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why do you store the key to refs.by_handle in refs.by_global_id instead of the NodeRef itself? I don\u0027t think there are any cases where we only need this intermediate result, so this makes refs.by_global_id always require two tree searches instead of one.",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d4185d5c_8c29fe7b",
        "filename": "drivers/android/process.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 421,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-04T11:11:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "With the current API of the red/black tree abstractions, the NodeRef must either be reference counted, or only stored in one tree. Making it reference counted introduces a third allocation, and also raises other challenges because then the type system no longer promises that we have an exclusive reference to the `NodeRef` when we look it up.\n\nBecause of that, I think this is the best solution until the red/black tree becomes more powerful.",
      "parentUuid": "c0abb748_f6f096da",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "84669a02_fcb864e8",
        "filename": "drivers/android/process.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 429,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-03T02:16:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why are these two separate extra allocations? We always need to insert the NodeRef (or a way to get to the NodeRef) into both trees and the C version does this without any extra allocation. For the linked list the rust version also seems to avoid allocating extra objects. Could we use a similar solution for the rbtrees?",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
